  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper low continues to move northeast this afternoon and evening, \nwith widespread rain observed across much of eastern North Dakota, \nwest central Minnesota, and northwest Minnesota. Winds have started \nto shift to the northeast, and will continue to become more \nnortherly through the evening hours and overnight. The upper low \nslowly exits the area late Tuesday into Wednesday morning, with \nbreezy north winds expected through much of Tuesday. Much cooler air \npours into the Northern Plains, with temperatures close to average \nvalues Tuesday through Friday. Our next chance for precipitation \nwill be late Saturday into Sunday as a fast moving trough moves \nacross the area.\n\n...Heavy Rain Through Wednesday...\n\nModerate to heavy rain will continue through the remainder of the \nafternoon and evening across much of eastern North Dakota, northwest \nMinnesota, and west central Minnesota. An upper low is slowly moving \nto the northeast, but is expected to occlude and briefly become cut \noff from the upper flow as we go through the day Tuesday. As this \nhappens, there could be a slight westward shift of the system. This \ncould keep light rain in the forecast through much of Tuesday and \ninto early Wednesday morning, especially east of the Red River \nValley. At this time, there is about a 75 percent chance to see an \nadditional 0.25-0.50 inch in the central Red River Valley, with \nhigher chances further east. Some locales could see upwards of 3 \ninches by Wednesday morning, with the best chances being in west \ncentral Minnesota (25 percent chance). \n\n...Breezy North Winds Tuesday...\n\nNorth winds fill into the area as the H5 low moves slowly eastward. \nThese winds will increase this evening and overnight, with 15-25 mph \nsustained winds expected by early Tuesday morning. Gusts up to 40 \nmph are possible, especially in the central and southern Red River \nValley. Pressure rises are expected to be sufficient to favor a few \nhigher gusts; however, with 700-850mb winds in the 35-45 knot range, \nand generally weak cold air advection, there is minimal support for \nanything more than occasional advisory level gusts.",
